Collections hold ordered groups of values. You create them with literals and work with them through built-in methods.

### Creating collections

Growable collections use `{T}`. Fixed collections use `{N T}` with a compile-time maximum length.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values ~= {1, 2, 3}
    empty_values ~{Int} = {}
    fixed_values ~{2 Int} = {}
]

Non-empty literals infer their element type from their items. Empty literals need an explicit collection type.

### Common methods

Collection methods use receiver syntax. `push`, `get`, `set` and `remove` take the fallible path, so they need `!` or `catch`. `length` stays infallible.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    values ~= {1, 2, 3}

    ~values.push(4) catch:
    ;

    first = values.get(0) catch:
        then 0
    ;

    ~values.set(1, 99) catch:
    ;

    count = values.length()
]

`push` appends after the last element. `get` reads at an index. `set` replaces an element. `remove` takes an element out and shifts later elements down. `length` returns the logical length, not the fixed capacity.

### Remember

- Use `{T}` for growable collections and `{N T}` for fixed collections.
- `push`, `get`, `set` and `remove` take the fallible path. Handle them with `!` or `catch`.
- `length` stays infallible.
- `set`, `push` and `remove` need `~` on the receiver.

### Read next

For the full language rules around collection literals, mutability and fixed capacity, see @../../../../collections (Collections). For the binding-backed contract behind these methods, see @collections (Core collections advanced).